{"name":"Purple Yam","hex":"#a846d7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"purple yam","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A yam used in a variety of desserts, Dioscorea alata."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/purple_yam"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dioscorea alata","blocks":[{"text":"Dioscorea alata – also called ube, ubi, uwhi, purple yam, winged yam, or greater yam, among many other names – is a species of yam. The tubers are usually a vivid violet-purple to bright lavender, but some range from creamy-white to plain white. Although it is sometimes confused with taro and the Okinawa sweet potato beniimo , D. alata is also grown in Okinawa. Its origins are in the Asian and Oceanian tropics. Some varieties attain great size. A mambatap greater yam grown in Maprik, East Sepik Province, Papua New Guinea, around 1939 was 3.5 m (11 ft) in length."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Kambar_wh.jpg/330px-Kambar_wh.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dioscorea_alata","wikidata":"Q1932852"},{"title":"Yam (vegetable)","blocks":[{"text":"Yam is the common name for some plant species in the genus Dioscorea that form edible tubers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Yam_at_monday_market_kaduna_state_01.jpg/330px-Yam_at_monday_market_kaduna_state_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yam_(vegetable)","wikidata":"Q8047551"},{"title":"Purple","blocks":[{"text":"Purple is a color similar in appearance to violet light. In the RYB color model historically used in the arts, purple is a secondary color created by combining red and blue pigments. In the CMYK color model used in modern printing, purple is made by combining magenta pigment with cyan pigment, black pigment, or both. In the RGB color model used in computer and television screens, purple is created by mixing red and blue light in order to create colors that appear similar to violet light. According to color theory, purple is considered a cool color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purple","wikidata":"Q3257809"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Ubejf1680.JPG/500px-Ubejf1680.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Ubejf1680.JPG/1280px-Ubejf1680.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Ubejf1680.JPG/500px-Ubejf1680.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Harvested purple yam tubers","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUbejf1680.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Starr_061106-9589_Dioscorea_alata.jpg/500px-Starr_061106-9589_Dioscorea_alata.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Starr_061106-9589_Dioscorea_alata.jpg/1280px-Starr_061106-9589_Dioscorea_alata.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Starr_061106-9589_Dioscorea_alata.jpg/500px-Starr_061106-9589_Dioscorea_alata.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aerial bulbil of a white variety of D. alata","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStarr_061106-9589_Dioscorea_alata.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Ube_